## Abstract

Autonomous software agents operating on enterprise codebases fail primarily due to **teleological drift**—the progressive loss of architectural intent across multi-step execution horizons. Current industry interventions attempt to mitigate this by expanding context windows, utilizing Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), or indexing Abstract Syntax Trees (ASTs). These techniques capture lexical similarity and syntactic relationships, but they fail to represent operational purpose, external stakeholder constraints, and system-level invariants.

We present **Metaform**, an agent-orientation system grounded in a **Recursive Tripartite Relational Ontology (RTAO)**. Metaform models workpieces not as static syntax trees or text tokens, but as dynamic, state-bifurcated hypergraphs of invariant functional purposes. By defining component identity as an invariant relational set rather than mutable implementation text, Metaform provides agents with an immutable coordinate system. This eliminates context-window exhaustion, prevents dependency hallucinations, and enables autonomous cross-stack mutation (e.g., automated C++ to Rust migration) with zero architectural regression.

